.TH FCP 1 "August 2026" "fcp 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fcp \- faster copy with progress, identical file detection, and parallelism
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fcp
[\fIOPTION\fR]... \fISOURCE\fR \fIDESTINATION\fR
.br
.B fcp
[\fIOPTION\fR]... \fISOURCE\fR... \fIDIRECTORY\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBfcp\fP is a faster replacement for the standard \fBcp\fP command with
enhanced features including visual progress bars, identical file detection,
parallel copy support, and more.

It is fully compatible with \fBcp\fP's command-line interface and adds
several new options for improved functionality.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-recursive\fR
Copy directories recursively.

.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-interactive\fR
Prompt before overwrite.

.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-no\-clobber\fR
Do not overwrite an existing file.

.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-force\fR
Remove existing destination files before copying.

.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Display names of copied files.

.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-dereference\fR
Copy the files that symlinks refer to, not the symlinks themselves.

.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-symbolic\fR
Create symbolic links instead of copying files.

.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-update\fR
Copy only when the source file is newer than the destination file.

.TP
\fB\-t\fR \fIDIRECTORY\fR, \fB\-\-target\-directory=\fIDIRECTORY\fR
Copy all sources into the specified DIRECTORY.

.TP
\fB\-P\fR, \fB\-\-progress\fR
Show progress bar during copy operations. Enabled by default when
outputting to a terminal.

.TP
\fB\-\-no\-progress\fR
Disable progress display.

.TP
\fB\-\-parallel=\fI[N|auto]\fR
Number of parallel copy workers.
.RS 4
.PP
\fBauto\fR (default): Automatically determine the number of workers
based on CPU count, capped at 8.
.PP
\fB1\fR: Sequential copy (traditional cp behavior).
.PP
\fIN\fR: Use exactly N worker threads.
.RE

.TP
\fB\-\-verify\-hash\fR
Use SHA256 hash comparison for identical file detection. By default,
fcp uses file size and modification time for fast detection. Enable
this option for cryptographically certain identical file detection.

.TP
\fB\-\-reflink=\fI[auto|always|never]\fR
Use reflink (FICLONE) when the filesystem supports it for instant
zero\-copy.
.RS 4
.PP
\fBauto\fR (default): Use reflink when supported, fall back to normal copy.
.PP
\fBalways\fR: Always attempt reflink (will fail on unsupported filesystems).
.PP
\fBnever\fR: Never use reflink.
.RE

.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Preview what would be copied without actually copying anything.
Shows the files that would be processed.

.TP
\fB\-\-speed\-limit=\fISIZE\fR
Cap the copy speed. SIZE can be a number with optional suffix:
.RS 4
.PP
\fBK\fR or \fBk\fR: Kilobytes (1024 bytes)
.PP
\fBM\fR or \fBm\fR: Megabytes (1024*1024 bytes)
.PP
\fBG\fR or \fBg\fR: Gigabytes (1024*1024*1024 bytes)
.PP
.PP
Example: \fB\-\-speed\-limit=50M\fR limits copy speed to 50 MB/s.
.RE

.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Disable colored output.

.TP
\fB\-\-config=\fIPATH\fR
Path to configuration file. Default: \fI~/.config/fcp/config\fR

.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help message and exit.

.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Show version information and exit.

.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
.PP
\fBfcp\fR reads configuration from \fI~/.config/fcp/config\fR (or the
path specified with \fB\-\-config\fR). The file uses a simple
key=value format:

.PP
.nf
    # fcp configuration file
    parallel = auto
    progress = auto
    color = auto
    verify_hash = off
    verbose = auto
    reflink = auto
    speed_limit =
.fi

.PP
CLI options override config file settings. The config file
settings override built-in defaults.

.SH IDENTICAL FILE DETECTION
.PP
\fBfcp\fR skips copying files that are already identical to their
destination. The detection uses a fast decision tree:

.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
Same inode and device: skip immediately (hardlinked files)
.IP \(bu 2
Different sizes: definitely different, copy
.IP \(bu 2
Same size and modification time: likely identical, skip
.IP \(bu 2
Same size, different time, \fB\-\-verify\-hash\fR: SHA256 compare
.RE

.PP
This approach is similar to rsync's strategy: use the cheapest
check first, escalating only when necessary.

.SH PERFORMANCE
.PP
\fBfcp\fR is optimized for speed through several mechanisms:

.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCopy file range\fR: Uses the \fBcopy_file_range()\fR syscall for
zero\-copy transfers when available (Linux 4.5+)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBParallel workers\fR: Multiple threads can copy different files
simultaneously
.IP \(bu 2
\fBReflinks\fR: On Btrfs/XFS, uses FICLONE for instant copies
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLarge buffers\fR: 1MB copy buffers reduce syscall overhead
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSequential I/O hint\fR: Advises the kernel for optimal read-ahead
.RE

.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Copy a single file:
.PP
.nf
    $ fcp source.txt destination.txt
.fi

.PP
Copy a directory recursively with progress:
.PP
.nf
    $ fcp \-r photos/ ~/backup/photos/
.fi

.PP
Parallel copy with 4 workers:
.PP
.nf
    $ fcp \-\-parallel=4 documents/ backup/documents/
.fi

.PP
Dry run to preview what would be copied:
.PP
.nf
    $ fcp \-\-dry\-run \-r src/ dest/
.fi

.PP
Copy with speed limit:
.PP
.nf
    $ fcp \-\-speed\-limit=100M large_file.iso ~/backup/
.fi

.PP
Copy multiple sources into a directory:
.PP
.nf
    $ fcp file1.txt file2.txt file3.txt destination_dir/
.fi

.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
.TP
\fBNO_COLOR\fR
If set to any value, disables colored output.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\fB0\fR
Successful completion.

.TP
\fB1\fR
An error occurred.

